import Mathlib

/-!
# Multiplier ideals of pairs and point separation: target signatures

This file is not the roadmap. The definitive document for this folder is `README.md`. This file
states the roadmap's headline theorems against the pinned Mathlib and Tau Ceti. None of them is
statable at the pins, so nothing is stated here.

Every headline is a statement about the positivity of adjoint divisors `K_X + Δ + N` on a
projective variety `X` over a field of characteristic zero: its hypotheses are klt or lc
conditions on a pair `(X, Δ)`, nefness and bigness of `ℚ`-Cartier divisors, and lower bounds for
the intersection numbers `(N^d · Z)` of a divisor with the subvarieties `Z` of every dimension `d`
(AlgebraicModuliForArithmeticGeometry §§1.6, 9.10; SchemeAndStackFoundations Layer 5). The pins
do not have these carriers. Tau Ceti has Weil divisors of a scheme with integer coefficients
(`TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.SchemeWeilDivisor`), Cartier divisors of an integral scheme
(`TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.CartierDivisor`), the canonical divisor of an integral curve
(`TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.relativeDifferentialsIsoDivisorSheaf`), and the intersection number
`TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.LineBundleClass.intersectionNumber` of a line bundle with a curve, which
is the case `d = 1` only. Neither Mathlib nor Tau Ceti has the canonical divisor of a normal
variety of higher dimension, `ℚ`-divisors with the `ℚ`-Cartier condition, nef or big divisors,
numerical equivalence, log resolutions, or the intersection numbers `(N^d · Z)` for `d ≥ 2`.
Defining these numbers through the growth of `h⁰` of powers would be a stand-in for the
intersection theory of SchemeAndStackFoundations Layer 5, not that theory.

* Nadel vanishing for pairs (9.10c): if `X` is a normal projective variety over a field of
  characteristic zero, `Δ` an effective `ℚ`-Weil divisor with `K_X + Δ` `ℚ`-Cartier and `L` a
  Cartier divisor with `L − (K_X + Δ)` nef and big, then `H^i(X, O_X(L) ⊗ J(X, Δ)) = 0` for every
  `i > 0`. It lacks the canonical Weil divisor of a normal variety, `ℚ`-Weil divisors with the
  `ℚ`-Cartier condition, nef and big `ℚ`-Cartier divisors, log resolutions with the `ℚ`-linear
  pullback and the round-up, and the multiplier ideal `J(X, Δ)` of a pair (9.10b), a push-forward
  from a log resolution. Tau Ceti has the cohomology `TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.Cohomology`
  of a sheaf of modules, so the vanishing itself would be expressible once these exist.
* Global generation of adjoint bundles (9.12b): over an algebraically closed field of
  characteristic zero, if `(X, Δ)` is a projective klt pair of dimension `n`, `N` a nef `ℚ`-Cartier
  divisor with `M = K_X + Δ + N` Cartier, `x` a closed point and `(N^d · Z) > (½n(n + 1))^d` for
  every subvariety `Z ∋ x` of positive dimension `d`, then `O_X(M)` is generated by global sections
  at `x`. It lacks, besides the carriers above, klt pairs (the discrepancies of 9.10a over all
  normal birational models) and the intersection numbers `(N^d · Z)` for `d ≥ 2`. Tau Ceti has
  global generation of an invertible sheaf
  (`TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.InvertibleSheaf.IsGloballyGenerated`), which with the nonvanishing
  locus of a section expresses generation at one point.
* Separation of points for adjoint bundles (9.12c): over such a field, if `X` is a smooth
  projective variety of dimension `n`, `L` an ample line bundle, `x_1, …, x_ℓ` distinct closed
  points and `(L^d · Z) > (½n(n + 2ℓ − 1))^d` for every subvariety `Z` of dimension `d ≥ 1`, then
  `H⁰(X, K_X + L)` maps onto `⊕_i (K_X + L) ⊗ k(x_i)`. Tau Ceti has ample line bundle classes
  (`TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.LineBundleClass.IsAmple`), the differentials of a smooth scheme as a
  finite locally free sheaf (`TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.FiniteLocallyFreeSheaf.relativeDifferentials`)
  and determinants of finite locally free sheaves of constant rank
  (`TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.FiniteLocallyFreeSheaf.determinant`), from which `K_X` is made. It
  lacks the intersection numbers `(L^d · Z)` of a line bundle with the closed integral subvarieties
  of dimension `d ≥ 2`, which the hypothesis needs for every `d` from `1` to `n`.
* Kollár's two-point separation (9.12a): over `ℂ`, if `(X, Δ)` is a projective klt pair of
  dimension `n ≥ 1`, `N` a nef and big `ℚ`-Cartier divisor, `x₁ ≠ x₂` closed points and
  `c(1), …, c(n) > 0` with `(N^e · Z) > c(e)^e` for every subvariety `Z` of dimension `e` through
  `x₁` or `x₂` (`Z = X` included) and `Σ_e 2^{1/e}·e/c(e) ≤ 1`, then every line bundle `M` whose
  degree on every complete curve is that of `K_X + Δ + N` has a global section vanishing at exactly
  one of `x₁`, `x₂`. It lacks klt pairs, nef and big `ℚ`-Cartier divisors, the degree of
  `K_X + Δ + N` on curves for a normal `X` (Tau Ceti's `LineBundleClass.intersectionNumber` gives
  the degree of a line bundle on a curve, not of a `ℚ`-Cartier divisor built from the canonical
  divisor), and the intersection numbers `(N^e · Z)` for `e ≥ 2`.

The case `n = 1` of the separation of points (on a smooth projective curve, `K_X + L` separates
`ℓ` points once `deg L > ℓ`) needs only degrees of line bundles on curves and the canonical
divisor of a curve, which Tau Ceti has; it is a weaker statement than the headline, which is
stated in every dimension, so it is not stated either. Nadel vanishing with `Δ = 0` on a smooth
variety is Kawamata–Viehweg vanishing, a target of AlgebraicModuliForArithmeticGeometry §9.10, not
of this roadmap. The Bertini theorem for a birational base-point-free linear system, division by
`D` sections with simple common zeros and the bound for sections by intersection numbers with a
generated line bundle (9.13, 9.13a, 9.13b) are statements used with the headlines, not headlines,
and they are not stated here.
-/

